description:
Build `Vec<T>`, a dynamic array like `std::vector`, that works even for types **without a default constructor**:

```cpp
template <class T>
class Vec
{
public:
    Vec();
    ~Vec();
    Vec(const Vec& other);
    Vec(Vec&& other) noexcept;
    Vec& operator=(const Vec& other);
    Vec& operator=(Vec&& other) noexcept;

    void push_back(const T& value);
    void pop_back();                       // throws std::out_of_range if empty
    void reserve(std::size_t capacity);    // never shrinks
    void insert(std::size_t index, const T& value);   // index may equal size(); otherwise std::out_of_range
    void erase(std::size_t index);                     // std::out_of_range if index >= size()

    T& operator[](std::size_t i);
    const T& operator[](std::size_t i) const;
    std::size_t size() const;
    std::size_t capacity() const;
    T* begin();  T* end();                 // so range-for works
};
```

- Growth: capacity 0 becomes 1, then doubles whenever a push or insert finds the array full.
- Use **raw storage**: allocate with `static_cast<T*>(::operator new(n * sizeof(T)))`, build elements in place with placement `new (ptr) T(value)`, destroy them with `ptr->~T()`, and free with `::operator delete(ptr)`. `new T[n]` would need a default constructor and would construct `n` elements you never asked for.
- When growing, **move** the elements into the new block (`std::move`), then destroy the old ones.
- Every element constructed must be destroyed exactly once, and nothing may leak (the tests count).

